RULE -> EVIDENCE mapping for the .gitattributes landed in the same ticket
==============================================================================
  (each rule of the landed .gitattributes and the scan class above it answers;
   the same mapping is repeated in-line as comments in the .gitattributes file)
  * text=auto eol=lf          <- L2/L3: 392 text files whose worktree carries CRLF
                                 while every stored blob is LF; pins checkout to LF
                                 regardless of the machine's core.autocrlf.
  -text for .png/.jpg/.f32/.npy <- L6: 110 NUL-bearing files (86 of them contain
                                 literal CRLF byte pairs, 100 contain lone CR) that a
                                 text filter would corrupt.

==============================================================================
RULE VALIDATION - measured after .gitattributes was written (same day)
==============================================================================
The scan above was taken BEFORE .gitattributes existed (note its `attr/` column is
empty in every row). Each rule was then validated in a throwaway scratch repository
(%TEMP%\b6-attr-a with the landed file, %TEMP%\b6-attr-b with only the wildcard
rule), so no file of this repository was written by the test.

Scratch setup: `git init` + `core.autocrlf true` (mimicking this clone) + payload
files named *.f32 carrying CRLF byte pairs; then `git add` and compare the stored
blob with the worktree bytes.

  payload (named *.f32)         case A: landed file   case B: `* text=auto eol=lf`
                                                      alone (control)
  ---------------------------   -------------------   ----------------------------
  printable bytes + CRLF        byte-identical        CRLF REMOVED (crlf 10 -> 0)
                                (crlf=10 kept)        => payload corrupted
  control bytes + CRLF + CR     byte-identical        byte-identical
                                (crlf + lone CR kept) (auto-detected as binary)

  => `* text=auto eol=lf` ALONE rewrites a tracked payload that git's NUL-based
     auto-detection reads as text; the first payload lost 10 line endings that way.
     Preventing exactly that is what rule 2 (`-text` on .png/.jpg/.npy/.f32) does:
     it is load-bearing, not decorative.
  => rule 1 verified from the other side: a CRLF text file was stored as LF and
     re-checked-out as LF (delete file + `git checkout-index -a -f`) even with
     core.autocrlf=true, i.e. eol=lf wins over the machine setting on both the add
     and the checkout side.
  => `-text` wins over the wildcard `eol=lf`: `git check-attr` reports `text: unset`
     for the binary paths and `text: auto, eol: lf` for text paths.

IN-REPO CHECKS after landing (read-only; no add, no commit):
  * `git check-attr -a` on 8 sampled paths: text/eol resolve exactly as designed.
  * `git status --short` right after landing: 12 entries = 2 modified + 10 untracked
    (that count moves while a concurrent writer is working). None of the 392
     worktree-CRLF files appear - a rule that forced renormalisation would have
     listed hundreds. (Those 2 modified files and the extra untracked logs belong
     to a concurrent writer, not to this ticket.)
  * limits of the change, stated plainly: these rules do NOT convert the existing
     worktree CRLF of those 392 files (that needs one fresh checkout), do NOT
     rewrite any stored object (every text blob was already LF, see the KEY READING
     block above), and do NOT stop a Windows tool from writing CRLF to disk - such
     bytes are simply normalised away at `git add` time, which is also why the drift
     never surfaces as a diff.

